MALLOC(3)                      P32W Unix                       MALLOC(3)

NAME
       malloc, free, calloc, realloc - memory allocator

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       void *malloc(size_t n);
       void free(void *p);
       void *calloc(size_t n, size_t size);
       void *realloc(void *p, size_t n);

DESCRIPTION
       malloc returns a block of at least n bytes (malloc(0) gives a
       block of its own too). Its contents are whatever was there
       before.

       free gives back a block from malloc, calloc or realloc, for
       reuse; free(NULL) does nothing.

       calloc returns a block for n objects of size bytes each, set
       to zeros.

       realloc changes the size of the block p to n bytes and returns
       it, perhaps moved: the contents are kept up to the smaller of
       the two sizes. realloc(NULL, n) is malloc(n). If n is no
       bigger than the block already is, realloc returns p as it
       stands (it never shrinks a block); otherwise it gets a new
       block, copies, and frees p.

       The allocator is the one of Kernighan and Ritchie: a circular
       list of free blocks in address order, searched first-fit from
       where the last search ended. Each block has an 8-byte header
       and is a multiple of 8 bytes. free merges a block with free
       neighbours. When nothing fits, malloc asks the kernel for at
       least 4096 more bytes with sbrk(2). Memory is never given
       back to the kernel.

       A process has 8 MiB of address space for its program, heap
       and stack (the stack may grow to 1 MiB); the heap can grow
       until it comes within a page of the stack.

RETURN VALUE
       malloc, calloc and realloc return the block, or NULL if sbrk
       fails (errno is then ENOMEM). When realloc fails, p is left
       as it was.

EXAMPLES
       char **v = malloc(n * sizeof *v);
       if (v == NULL) { fprintf(stderr, "out of memory\n"); exit(1); }
       ...
       v = realloc(v, 2 * n * sizeof *v);

SEE ALSO
       sbrk(2), strdup(3), stdlib(3)

BUGS
       Nothing checks the pointers given to free and realloc: a
       pointer not from malloc, or freed twice, corrupts the free
       list, as does writing past the end of a block. calloc does
       not check n * size for overflow. A block freed while the
       heap is mostly in use makes no memory available to other
       processes.
